# Web and Android Feature Parity Rules

## Intent
Ensure every user-facing feature implemented in web is also implemented in Android with equivalent core behavior and safety.

## Scope
- Applies to `packages/web` and `packages/mobile`.
- There is **no native iOS app** — native iOS delivery remains out of scope. iOS users are served by the **responsive web app** (see "Responsive web" below). "web↔Android parity" in this rule means feature parity between the Next.js web app and the React Native Android app; it does not imply a native iOS build.

## Responsive web (serving iOS and every mobile browser)
- The web app (`packages/web`) must be **responsive**: it renders the **Desktop** design at wide viewports and the **Mobile** design at narrow (phone) viewports, in the **same** web components (responsive layout / CSS — not a separate route, app, or component set). This is how iOS Safari and every mobile-browser user accesses the product.
- The `Mobile*` design mockups are the **single source of truth for both** the Android native app **and** the web's mobile breakpoint. A phone layout is the same whether React Native or a ~390px browser paints it. **Do not commission a separate "web responsive" mockup series — there is nothing new to design.** The design agent authors Desktop + `Mobile*` mockups only.
- Building the web's mobile-responsive layout from the existing `Mobile*` design is an **app / front-end (non-design-agent) responsibility**. A phone breakpoint is not a new design surface and is **not** design-gated by rule 127.
- Therefore every user-facing UI change lands all three together: the desktop-web layout (Desktop mockup), the **mobile-web responsive layout in the same web components** (Mobile mockup), and the Android implementation (Mobile mockup). Shipping a web feature that only works at desktop width — leaving iOS/mobile-web users a broken or desktop-only view — is a parity gap, not "done".

## Mandatory — 100% Parity Baseline

100% web↔Android parity is the baseline state for every user-facing plugin and platform feature. Parity is not a goal, a roadmap item, or a future state — it is a precondition for landing user-facing work.

- Any new web user-facing feature must ship its Android implementation in the same PR (or in a strictly coordinated PR pair merged together).
- Any new Android user-facing feature must ship its web implementation in the same PR (or in a strictly coordinated PR pair merged together).
- Refactors, behavioral changes, and bug fixes on user-facing surfaces must update both platforms in the same change.
- Parity is required for all user-facing features: plugin messaging, plugin commands, account settings, consent/privacy controls, safety-critical flows, navigation, and shell chrome.
- Differences in UI layout are allowed when platform conventions require it, but behavior and outcomes must match.

## Delivery Rules
- PR descriptions must declare `Parity Status: web + mobile-responsive + android complete`. The CI parity gate enforces this. The three parts are the desktop web layout, the same web app at a phone-width (mobile-responsive) breakpoint, and the Android app — a user-facing UI change is only "complete" when the phone-width web layout is done too, not just desktop. (The older `web+android complete` form is still accepted so existing PRs don't break.)
- The `Parity Ticket: <issue-or-URL>` escape hatch is reserved for explicit, time-boxed exceptions only. It is not a default option and is not appropriate for new feature work.
- An exception must include: owner, justification, expiry date, and risk acceptance from the platform owner.
- An exception is invalid for safety, privacy, compliance, or trauma-informed features — those must be delivered at parity, period.

## No Phased Rollouts
- Features are either delivered or not delivered. "Phase 0", "Phase 1", and "we will add Android later" framings are prohibited in PRs, inventories, checklists, design artifacts, and code comments.
- If a feature cannot be delivered on both platforms in the same change, the feature is not ready to land.
- This is the parity-specific case of the general no-phases rule in `CLAUDE.md` → "Task Planning — No Phases": break work into an ordered, dependency-based task list, never phase buckets.

## Testing Rules
- Add parity acceptance criteria for critical paths in test plans.
- Validate both web and Android for accessibility and trauma-informed constraints when introducing new user workflows.

## Prohibited
- Shipping web-only or Android-only experiences for any user-facing flow without an approved exception (see Delivery Rules).
- Marking parity complete when only UI shell exists and core behavior is missing.
- Using phase language to justify a parity gap.
